  "image_name": "930bad7f16c4c1a6827fcd4d4e0e3207.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic map linking four kinds of transport in spin caloritronics: charge current, electron-spin current, magnon-spin current, and heat current. Each quantity is drawn in a corner, and arrows between corners indicate how one type of flow can be converted into another.\n\nOn the left, charge and electron-spin currents are coupled by the spin Hall effect and its inverse, showing mutual conversion between electrical current and spin-polarized electron flow. On the right, heat current and magnon-spin current are similarly connected by the spin Seebeck and spin Peltier effects, indicating that thermal gradients can generate spin-wave transport and vice versa. Across the top, electron-spin and magnon-spin currents are linked by spin-mixing conductance, representing exchange across an interface.\n\nDiagonal arrows highlight additional cross-couplings between spin and heat or charge, including the spin-dependent Seebeck and Peltier effects, spin Ettingshausen effect, and spin Nernst effect. Along the bottom, heat and charge are connected by the ordinary Seebeck–Peltier effect and a magneto-thermoelectric effect. Overall, the image summarizes how electrical, thermal, and spin excitations are interconverted in magnetic materials and interfaces.",
